      "id": 1,
      "text": "Westbrook first settled around 1648 as the Oyster River Quarter of Old Saybrook, founded by Puritans fleeing Charles I’s reign.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.westbrookct.us/319/History-of-Westbrook-Ct",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s original name, Pochaug, comes from an Algonquian word meaning “at the confluence of two rivers.”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.westbrookct.us/319/History-of-Westbrook-Ct",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Westbrook’s shipbuilding boom relied on local white oak and chestnut forests, producing vessels for nearly a century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.westbrookct.us/319/History-of-Westbrook-Ct",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Turtle, the world’s first submarine, was invented by Westbrook native David Bushnell and is linked to the town’s maritime heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.westbrookct.us/319/History-of-Westbrook-Ct",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Westbrook Town Center Historic District spans 178 acres, preserving the community’s architectural legacy at the heart of the town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Westbrook_Center%2C_Connecticut",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
